.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_ADD 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_add \- register a selectable with an event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_reg ccol_event_loop_add(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_selectable sel,
                       ccol_event_handlers_t handlers, void *arg,
                       char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function registers sel with loop. The loop then dispatches the
readiness of sel through handlers. It does this for all the life of the
registration, or until a call to ccol_event_loop_remove().

You build sel exactly as ccol_select() builds one. You can use
ccol_selectable_from_fd, ccol_selectable_from_circq,
ccol_selectable_from_dynq, and ccol_selectable_from_chan directly.

One registration covers exactly one direction, which is sel.dir. A caller
can want both directions live on one fd at the same time. One example is a
full-duplex pipe that a program reads and writes together. Such a caller
calls ccol_event_loop_add() two times and gets two independent handles. A
caller can also want to change the direction of one registration over time.
One example is a socket that connects: it wants write interest until the
connect finishes, and read interest after that. Such a caller uses one
registration and ccol_event_loop_modify(3). The function refuses a second
selectable for a direction that is already in use on the same fd. Two read
registrations on one fd is one such case. The call then returns
CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID, and err_str names that cause; see RETURN VALUE.

A queue selectable and a ccol_channel selectable have no such restriction.
You can make any number of ccol_event_loop_add() calls for the same queue
and the same direction, in one ccol_event_loop or in several. You can also
mix those with a ccol_select() call that runs at the same time on the same
queue and the same direction. All of them are live together, and each one
gets a turn. This is the same guarantee that ccol_select() gives: any
number of threads can watch one queue at the same time.

A message can arrive while more than one such listener is registered. For
each producer call and each consumer call, the library wakes exactly one
listener. After the callback of the woken listener returns, that listener
checks whether the queue is still ready for its direction. If it is, the
listener passes the wake to the next listener in the line. No live listener
is therefore passed over for ever. A ccol_select() caller that wakes and
then leaves without an action on the queue passes the wake in the same way,
and so does a registration that is removed, or whose loop is destroyed,
before it acted on its wake. No wake is therefore lost.

A registration whose callback moved at least one message is dispatched
again for as long as the queue stays ready for its direction: for a read
registration the queue holds fewer messages after the callback than before
it, and for a write registration it holds more. This holds for each
registration on its own, wherever it sits in the line and whatever the
other listeners of the queue do. A backlog therefore drains without another
send or receive, even when each callback receives one message, and a
listener that declines the queue never holds up one that takes it. A
registration whose callback neither receives nor sends passes the wake on,
and a wake that every listener declines stops once it has visited each of
them, so listeners that ignore a ready queue do not spin. A write
registration whose callback sends on every call is dispatched again for as
long as the queue has room, exactly as a writable fd is; remove it when it
has nothing to send.

A queue that you register this way must live longer than its registration.
ccol_circular_queue_destroy(3), ccol_dynamic_queue_destroy(3), and
ccol_channel_destroy(3) assert if a live registration still watches the
queue. They also assert if a ccol_select() call or a ccol_select_timed()
call still watches it. Without those asserts, the waiter is still linked
and points to the mutex of the queue, which the destroy is about to free.
Call ccol_event_loop_remove() before you destroy the queue. A destroy of
the loop first is fine, because ccol_event_loop_destroy() and
ccol_event_loop_remove() always unlink the waiter of a queue before
anything touches it again. ccol_event_loop_remove() returns without a wait
for a callback that it already gave to a reactor thread. The destroy that
comes after it therefore blocks until any such callback finishes with the
queue. The page of that destroy names the two things that a caller must not
do while it waits.

on_readable, on_writable and on_error share one prototype:

    void fn(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg,
            ccol_selectable *sel, void *arg);

loop is the loop that owns the registration, sel is its selectable, and
arg is the arg of this call. reg is the handle of the registration itself,
exactly the value that ccol_event_loop_add() returns for it. A callback can
pass reg to ccol_event_loop_remove(3), ccol_event_loop_pause(3),
ccol_event_loop_resume(3), ccol_event_loop_modify(3) and
ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(3). The registration is live, and a callback
of it can run, before ccol_event_loop_add() returns. With
num_reactor_threads > 1 a callback can run on a dispatch worker while the
calling thread is still inside this function, for example when the fd is
already ready. reg is valid in that callback too, so a callback never needs
to read the handle from storage that the calling thread writes after this
call returns.

handlers.on_readable reports readiness only, for every type of selectable.
ccol_event_loop never does the receive itself. For an fd selectable, the
callback makes its own read(2) or recv(2) call on sel->fd. For a queue
selectable or a ccol_channel selectable, the callback makes its own
ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() or ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc() call on sel->cq or
sel->dq. For a ccol_channel selectable that is ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() on
sel->cq, and never ccol_chan_try_recv_zc(): the callback runs on a thread
of the loop, which the channel counts as a worker, so a ccol_chan_* call
there can reach the other queue of the channel. See
ccol_selectable_from_chan(3). In both cases another consumer can already
hold the data. That
TOCTOU race is part of the design, and it is the same contract as the write
direction of ccol_select(). The receive call of the callback can therefore
find nothing, and the callback must handle that well.

For a queue selectable, one notification does not always mean exactly one
message. Several sends can run faster than the dispatch, and the callback
then sees them as one notification. A callback that receives one message
for each call still drains a burst, because the loop dispatches the
listeners again while the queue stays ready after a callback that moved a
message; see above. A callback can also receive in a loop until the call
returns ccol_container_empty, which drains a burst in fewer dispatches.

handlers.on_writable runs with no payload, for both kinds of selectable.
The caller makes its own write(2) or send(2) call. For a queue, it makes
its own try-send call, which does not block: ccol_circq_try_send_zc() on
sel->cq for a circular queue or a ccol_channel selectable, and
ccol_dynmq_send_zc() on sel->dq for a dynamic queue.

handlers.on_error runs only for an fd selectable. It runs for each
condition that the direction of the registration has no other handler for.
EPOLLERR and EPOLLHUP go here whenever the direction is not also ready.
For a read registration with
on_readable set to NULL, the close or the shutdown of the end of the peer
also goes here. That is EPOLLRDHUP, which an ordinary TCP close(2) raises
without EPOLLHUP.

If the direction does have its own handler, that handler wins the same
event whenever the direction is also ready. Data can arrive together with
the error. A read registration with
on_readable set then gets on_readable, and not on_error. EPOLLIN and
EPOLLRDHUP are not mutually exclusive with EPOLLERR and EPOLLHUP, and the
loop still delivers the bytes that arrived before the peer went away. For
the same reason, a write registration with on_writable set gets
on_writable. EPOLLOUT is also not mutually exclusive with EPOLLERR and
EPOLLHUP. A socket in an error state is writable too, because a write(2)
call on it returns an error immediately and does not block. A queue
selectable never makes an error condition.

A registration with on_error set to NULL gets EPOLLERR and EPOLLHUP through
the handler of its own direction instead, as libevent and libuv deliver
them: on_readable for a read registration, on_writable for a write
registration. The read(2), recv(2), write(2) or send(2) call of that
handler then reports the condition: -1 with the error in errno, or 0 for an
end of file. A read or a write of a socket also clears a pending socket
error. A transient error, such as the ICMP port unreachable that a
connected UDP socket reports as ECONNREFUSED, therefore costs one failed
receive, and the datagrams that arrive after it still reach on_readable.
When both directions of one fd have such a handler, one event reaches both:
on_readable first, then on_writable, one after the other on one thread, and
each sees whatever the other left of the condition.

Each of on_readable, on_writable, and on_error can be NULL. For an fd
selectable, the loop does not arm a direction that has no handler of its
own. A registration can carry on_error alone and leave the handler
of its own direction NULL. Such an error-only registration costs no
dispatch until something goes wrong. This is true in both directions. It
includes an error-only registration on the write direction of a healthy
socket, which is writable from the moment of its registration.

Every condition here is level-triggered, and an error is one of them. The
loop reports it again and again, and it calls the handler again and again.
This continues until a call removes or pauses the registration, or until
the condition clears. A hang-up stays true: a pipe whose writer
closed, or a socket whose peer is gone, keeps reporting it, and a read(2)
of it keeps returning 0. A handler that reads an end of file must remove
the registration (or pause it), exactly as it must for any other
readiness that it leaves in place. Remove it before you close the fd; see
ccol_event_loop_remove(3).

An fd event that no handler of the registration can take is dropped, and
the loop also stops watching that registration, because the hang-up behind
it can stay true and would otherwise be reported on every wait. Only a
registration with neither the handler of its direction nor on_error gets
there, for example a read registration with both on_readable and on_error
set to NULL whose peer hangs up. The registration stays registered, and a
registration for the other direction
of the same fd stays watched. ccol_event_loop_resume(3) and a successful
ccol_event_loop_modify(3) arm it again, whether the modify changes the
direction or keeps it.

handlers.on_removed is not tied to a readiness event, which makes it
different from the other three. It runs exactly once, at the moment when
the library can prove that the caller can free arg. After
ccol_event_loop_remove() it runs asynchronously, never from inside that
call. ccol_event_loop_destroy(3) runs every on_removed that is still due
from inside the call, on the destroying thread, before it returns. The
NOTES of ccol_event_loop_remove(3) give the exact guarantee. They also
explain why ccol_event_loop_remove() itself cannot always give it
synchronously. You can leave on_removed NULL for a registration whose arg
needs no notice before a free. A call of ccol_event_loop_destroy(3) on the
owning loop from on_removed is fatal: on an ordinary removal on_removed
runs on the reactor thread, where a destroy of its own loop is refused, and
during a destroy the handle is already destroyed.

A callback runs on one of the background reactor threads of loop. It never
runs on the thread that calls this function. The time that a callback can
take depends on the configuration of the loop. With the default of one
reactor thread, that one thread polls and runs the callbacks. A callback
that blocks therefore stalls every other registration on the loop for all
that time. Keep such a callback short and give slow work to a thread of
your own. With more than one reactor thread, there is one dedicated poller
and the callbacks run on a separate dispatch pool. That is the purpose of
that configuration, so a callback can take real time and the polling does
not starve. A callback holds no internal lock of the loop while it runs. It
can therefore take an application lock of its own. It can also call
ccol_event_loop_modify(3), ccol_event_loop_pause(3) and
ccol_event_loop_remove(3). Those calls keep working while
ccol_event_loop_shutdown(3) or ccol_event_loop_destroy(3) drains the
loop, because loop stays a live handle until that drain ends. A callback
that removes every registration of its fd and then frees arg is
therefore safe during a destroy on another thread: no callback of those
registrations runs after the removals return.

With more than one reactor thread, the loop still never runs the callback
of one registration at the same time as itself. See num_reactor_threads in
ccol_event_loop_create_with_mprocs(3). A read registration and a write
registration that share one fd also never run at the same time as each
other. A pair of callbacks that shares state across both directions of one
fd therefore needs no lock of its own for that reason. One TLS connection
object is such a pair. This rule does not reach two different fds. The
callbacks of different registrations can run at the same time on different
reactor threads.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop      ccol_event_loop to register with.
sel       What to watch. See DESCRIPTION.
handlers  Group of callbacks. Each callback can be NULL.
arg       Opaque pointer that the loop gives to every callback of this
          registration.
err_str   Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure, and
          NULL on success.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the function gives a new registration handle.
On failure, it gives CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID. The call fails in these
cases. loop is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, or loop is a stale handle or a
handle that a call already destroyed. Or sel is malformed, because its
type or its dir is not valid, its queue pointer is NULL, or its fd is
negative. Or the direction is already in use on that fd. Or an
allocation, or a system call (epoll_ctl or kevent, eventfd, or the
initialisation of a mutex), failed. An fd that names a regular file or
a directory is always refused this way, because epoll(7) cannot watch
such a file (epoll_ctl(2) fails with EPERM); the FreeBSD build refuses
it the same way, although kqueue(2) could watch it, so that one program
behaves alike on both systems.

When err_str is not NULL, *err_str names the cause, one message for each
class. A direction already in use gives a message that contains
"direction already in use"; no retry can succeed there. A failed
allocation gives one that contains "failed to allocate", and a failed
system call gives one that contains "a system call failed".
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
ccol_event_reg is an opaque VALUE handle, as ccol_event_loop itself is. It
is not a pointer. Never cast it to void* or from void*. Never compare it
through a pointer cast. Never treat it as an address. Compare it against
CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID. You can also use it in a truthiness check, because
CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID is 0. The library resolves every use of a
ccol_event_reg against the registration table of its own loop before it
dereferences anything. It therefore always finds a stale handle, or a
handle that a call already removed, and it refuses it cleanly. It is never
a use-after-free. This is also true while another thread races a
ccol_event_loop_modify, ccol_event_loop_pause, ccol_event_loop_resume,
ccol_event_loop_remove, or ccol_event_loop_reg_generation call on the very
same registration.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
You can call this function at the same time as ccol_event_loop_remove()
and ccol_event_loop_modify(). You can also call it from inside a callback
that runs on the reactor thread.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void on_msg(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg, ccol_selectable *sel,
            void *arg) {
    c_message_t msg;
    if (ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(sel->cq, &msg) != ccol_success) return;
    printf("job: %.*s\en", (int)msg.size, (char *)msg.data);
    free(msg.data);
}

ccol_circular_queue *jobs = ccol_circular_queue_create(64, NULL);
ccol_event_handlers_t handlers = { .on_readable = on_msg };
ccol_event_reg reg = ccol_event_loop_add(
    loop, ccol_selectable_from_circq(jobs, ccol_select_read),
                               handlers, NULL, NULL);
.fi
